WEBVTT

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:01.816
Hi, I'm Transom!

00:00:01.816 --> 00:00:06.231
A stochastic flow map jumps a noisy process forward in a few big steps.

00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:08.754
Here's how it got the best training loss.

00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:12.145
Brownian motion has no slope anywhere.

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:15.742
So replace it with a smooth polynomial that converges to it,

00:00:15.742 --> 00:00:17.050
and train against that.

00:00:19.692 --> 00:00:22.090
The first paper used a semigroup loss.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.831
One big step must equal two half steps.

00:00:24.831 --> 00:00:25.859
No derivative needed.

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:30.981
The new paper trains the flow map of a random ordinary differential equation.

00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:34.236
Its slope exists, and every coefficient enters the loss.

00:00:36.275 --> 00:00:38.174
Here is the equation the map now learns.

00:00:38.594 --> 00:00:40.554
The first term is the usual drift.

00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:44.804
The second is the slope of the polynomial path.

00:00:44.804 --> 00:00:48.763
As the degree grows, the solution converges to the true stochastic one.

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:54.116
Summarise the noise on the step as three Legendre coefficients.

00:00:54.536 --> 00:00:57.991
They enter a pretrained flow model through a residual that starts at

00:00:57.991 --> 00:01:00.294
zero, so the model begins as its teacher.

00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:03.792
The noise term is computed exactly, from the same path, not

00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:04.911
learned by a head.

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:08.941
Drift plus exact noise gives the next state, in one network call.

00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:13.425
Top, the smooth path, where a derivative loss exists.

00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:16.806
Bottom, the rough path, where only the semigroup loss works.

00:01:17.226 --> 00:01:21.494
On ImageNet, the improved map sits below the original at every step count.

00:01:22.758 --> 00:01:26.623
At four steps, the new parameterisation and the new loss each roughly

00:01:26.623 --> 00:01:30.165
halve the error, against the authors' own port of the original.

00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:35.215
Turn the noise into an input the derivative can see, and a

00:01:35.215 --> 00:01:37.372
stochastic map trains like a deterministic one.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:43.450
A polynomial path, a derivative loss, and exact noise.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.594
That's the improved strong flow map.

00:01:46.014 --> 00:01:48.202
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:48.202 --> 00:01:48.827
I'm Transom.

00:01:48.827 --> 00:01:49.140
Bye!
